From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HeDo <ldileo@digipa.it>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:48:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] LocalOptimization does the operations of equal priority in
 the order they were made

The heap of the local optimization ordered its elements by priority only, so the order of two
operations with the same priority was the one the heap functions of the standard library left,
which is not the same in libstdc++ and in the library of MSVC. A flat region, where every edge
collapse costs 0, was then simplified differently by programs built with each. Each element now
carries the number of its creation, taken from a counter that increases over the whole program,
and of two equal priorities the element made first comes first: the operations are done in the
same order with every library.
---
 vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization.h | 20 ++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization.h b/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization.h
index fb91456..b3c77af 100644
--- a/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization.h
+++ b/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization.h
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #ifndef __VCGLIB_LOCALOPTIMIZATION
 #define __VCGLIB_LOCALOPTIMIZATION
 #include <vcg/complex/complex.h>
+#include <atomic>
 #include <time.h>
 namespace vcg{
 // Base class for Parameters
@@ -167,19 +168,34 @@ public:
     ///pointer to instance of local modifier
     LocModType *locModPtr;
     float pri;
+    /// When the element was made: of two operations with the same priority, the one made first
+    /// is done first. Without it the order of equal priorities is the one the heap functions of
+    /// the standard library leave, which is not the same in every library (a flat region, where
+    /// every collapse costs 0, would be simplified differently by each).
+    unsigned long long serial;
    
     inline HeapElem( LocModType *_locModPtr)
     {
       locModPtr = _locModPtr;
       pri=float(locModPtr->Priority());
+      serial=NextSerial();
+    }
+
+    /// Increasing over the whole program, so that it is increasing in each heap whichever
+    /// thread fills it; only the order of the numbers of one heap matters.
+    static unsigned long long NextSerial()
+    {
+      static std::atomic<unsigned long long> next(0);
+      return next.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
     }
 
     /// STL heap has the largest element as the first one.
     /// usually we mean priority as an error so we should invert the comparison
     inline bool operator <(const HeapElem & h) const
     { 
-		  return (pri > h.pri);
-		  //return (locModPtr->Priority() < h.locModPtr->Priority());
+		  if (pri != h.pri)
+		    return (pri > h.pri);
+		  return (serial > h.serial);
 	  }
 
     bool IsUpToDate() const
